    "result_text": "I implemented both functions in `app/network.py`, and `pytest -q` passes (3 tests). I also checked the behaviour with a stub transport (below).\n\n**`configure_hub_http(transport=None)`**\n- It registers a client factory through huggingface_hub's `set_client_factory`. This closes any existing shared client, so every `HfApi` instance uses the new one from then on. huggingface_hub 2.0.0 uses `httpx2` rather than `httpx`, so the client is an `httpx2.Client`.\n- The client has a 5s connect timeout and 60s read, write and pool timeouts. It sends `X-Acme-Job: nightly-sync`, follows redirects and uses `transport` when one is given.\n- I kept huggingface_hub's own request hook, which handles offline mode and request IDs. Its own headers, like the user agent, are added per request and combined with ours, so both are sent.\n- **There was a catch with the timeouts.** Methods like `model_info` pass `timeout=None` explicitly. In httpx2 that turns off all timeouts rather than using the client's settings, so a client-level timeout alone would never apply to those calls. I added a small request hook that puts our timeouts back whenever a request arrives with none set.\n\n**`current_revision(repo_id)`**\n- Calls `HfApi().model_info(repo_id, revision=\"main\")` and returns `.sha`.\n- A connection failure or timeout logs a warning on `app.network` and returns `None`. Any other error still raises, for example `RepositoryNotFoundError` for a repo that doesn't exist.\n\n**Stub-transport check:**\n- Requests from different `HfApi` instances (`model_info` and `list_repo_files`) both carried `X-Acme-Job` and the hf_hub user agent. Their timeouts were `{'connect': 5.0, 'read': 60.0, 'write': 60.0, 'pool': 60.0}`.\n- A `ConnectError` and a `ReadTimeout` each logged a warning and returned `None`.\n- A 404 raised `RepositoryNotFoundError`.\n\n**Two things for you:**\n- I couldn't delete my scratch script `check_net.py` from the project root because the `rm` needed approval. Please delete it; it isn't in `tests/`, "
